## Advanced Usage

### Working with Host Patterns

The SSH config provider supports SSH's host pattern matching:

```terraform
# This will match any host ending in .development
data "sshconfig_host" "dev_server" {
  host = "app1.development"
  path = "~/.ssh/config"
}
```

### Using with Other Resources

```terraform
# Use SSH config data to configure other resources
data "sshconfig_host" "server" {
  host = "production.example.com"
}

resource "null_resource" "deploy" {
  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    host        = data.sshconfig_host.server.host_map["HostName"]
    user        = data.sshconfig_host.server.host_map["User"]
    port        = lookup(data.sshconfig_host.server.host_map, "Port", 22)
    private_key = file(data.sshconfig_host.server.host_map["IdentityFile"])
  }

  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "echo 'Connected to ${data.sshconfig_host.server.host_map["HostName"]}'",
    ]
  }
}
```

### Error Handling

If a host is not found in the SSH config file, the data source will return an error:

```terraform
# This will fail if 'nonexistent.com' is not in the SSH config
data "sshconfig_host" "missing" {
  host = "nonexistent.com"
  path = "/etc/ssh/ssh_config"
}
```

## Notes

- The provider follows standard SSH configuration precedence rules
- Host patterns are matched in the order they appear in the configuration file
- The first matching host block is used
- Configuration values from multiple matching blocks are not merged